# transformers went with it. Its only use was FinBERT sentiment scoring, which
# needs torch and therefore failed to load on every production run since Phase 0
# ("Error loading FinBERT: name 'torch' is not defined"), scoring nothing while
# the dashboard displayed a permanently-neutral gauge. Headlines are now stored
# unscored — see pipeline/sentiment.py. Restoring a scorer is a Phase 2 item and
# needs a dated news archive before it can be backtested at all.
#
# torch was removed in Phase 0. It was needed only by the LSTM, which never
# wrote a checkpoint and so never produced a forecast; that module now lives in
# pipeline/archived/ and is not imported anywhere on the live path. Dropping it
# also removes the largest contributor to memory pressure on the free tier.
# Reinstall it if experiment E3 brings the ensemble back.

# streamlit and plotly went with the app/ directory. The Streamlit dashboard was
# retired once the Next.js frontend took over; it was the ONLY consumer of
# either package, and both were being installed on Render — which serves the
# API and has never rendered a chart. plotly alone is ~15MB on an instance that
# has already been OOM-killed once.

# lxml — REQUIRED although nothing declares it, found by the first locked CI run
# (2026-09-22). Production had been resolving yfinance 1.7.0, which depends on
# lxml; the lock pins the researched yfinance 1.3.0, which does NOT, so the
# locked runner had no lxml and yf.Ticker().earnings_dates — pandas.read_html
# under the hood — raised for all 84 tickers. compute_earnings_surprise catches
# that and writes 0.0, so arnings_surprise, one of the 15 pooled FACTORS,
# silently became a constant-zero column on every row the job rewrote. A failed
# measurement stored as a valid neutral value, which is the FinBERT / FII-flow
# landmine for the third time. Pinned here so the environment cannot lose it.
lxml>=4.9.0
